Foreign Body Removal (Skin, Eye, ENT, soft tissue)
General Practice Other
History of penetrating injury/object insertion, Localized acute pain/irritation, Visible object/unilateral purulent discharge, Immediate relief upon extraction

VISIT CONTEXT
Case ID: GPO-016
Reason for Visit: Acute, escalating distress and localized soft tissue infection secondary to a deeply retained, contaminated foreign body.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient, parent
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported a pediatric patient with a clear history of a penetrating injury occurring earlier today, presenting tonight with rapidly escalating localized acute pain, irritation, and early signs of secondary infection. Urgent face-to-face assessment was strictly required to visualize the object, perform an immediate surgical extraction under local anesthesia, and initiate prophylactic antibiotics to prevent deep tissue abscess formation or systemic spread overnight. Delaying this assessment until the next in-hours period would have subjected the child to severe, unmanageable pain and a significantly heightened risk of complicated soft tissue infection requiring surgical admission.

SUBJECTIVE
Chief Complaint: "My son stepped on a massive piece of rusty wire in the yard, it's stuck deep in his foot, and he is screaming if anyone touches it."
History of Present Illness: The patient presents with a clear history of a penetrating injury from a contaminated environmental source (rusty fencing wire) sustained approximately 6 hours ago. The patient reports localized acute pain, irritation, and a sharp foreign body sensation upon any attempt to bear weight. The parent reports a visible protruding object such as a metal shard in the skin of the right plantar arch, which they were unable to extract manually. There is localized erythema, swelling, and tenderness indicating early secondary infection around the retained skin object. The pain is escalating, rendering the patient entirely non-weight-bearing and highly distressed.
Functional Status: Non-weight-bearing on the affected limb due to severe pain.
Social/Safety Concerns: High anxiety in both patient and parent; risk of tetanus from a dirty, penetrating yard wound.

OBJECTIVE
Vitals: BP: 110/70 | HR: 115 | Temp: 37.4 | RR: 20 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 28 Kg
General: Tearful, guarding the right foot, distressed but consolable by parent.
Physical Exam: Integumentary: Examination of the right plantar surface reveals a 2mm puncture wound with a visible, protruding metallic foreign body. There is a 1.5cm radius of surrounding erythema and induration. Extreme hyperalgesia surrounds the wound. The clinical pathway requires the application of local or topical anaesthesia to facilitate removal, anticipating relief of symptoms almost immediately upon successful extraction. Expected investigation results include direct clinical visualization of the object, which in this case confirms the diagnosis without the need for plain soft tissue radiography.
Environmental Observations: Clean living room environment; parent has appropriately elevated the limb prior to arrival.
Medication Reconciliation: Paracetamol 15mg/kg administered by parent 1 hour prior.

ASSESSMENT
Primary Diagnosis: Foreign Body Removal (Skin, Eye, ENT, soft tissue) (Plantar Puncture Wound) - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Acute Cellulitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The presence of a definitively visible retained foreign body acting as the mechanical source of the localized erythema, rather than a primary spreading bacterial infection.

Osteomyelitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The acute timeline (6 hours) of the injury, which is insufficient for bone infection to develop, and no systemic signs of sepsis.

Tendon Rupture - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Intact active plantar and dorsiflexion of all toes, despite the pain.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY
The patient sustained a contaminated, deep puncture wound with a retained foreign body, resulting in acute hyperalgesia, localized swelling, and an absolute inability to bear weight. Telephone triage correctly identified the high risk of rapid infection progression (e.g., Pseudomonas or Staphylococcus) from a deep plantar wound. Urgent face-to-face evaluation was clinically mandated to perform an immediate digital nerve block for analgesia, mechanically extract the contaminated foreign body, irrigate the tract, and administer a tetanus booster. Deferring this intervention until the next in-hours period would have resulted in an enclosed, contaminated puncture wound evolving into an acute abscess overnight, likely requiring formal operative debridement.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN
Clinical Interventions: Administered a local anesthetic block (1% Lidocaine without epinephrine). Successfully grasped and extracted a 1.5cm rusted metallic wire fragment using sterile forceps. Copiously irrigated the deep wound tract with sterile saline. Administered a Tetanus toxoid booster (IM deltoid) and prescribed oral Cephalexin 25mg/kg QID for 5 days.
Positive Behavioral Change: To ensure the puncture wound heals without closing over trapped bacteria, strictly perform warm saltwater foot soaks twice daily for the next three days. Pair the foot soak directly with the child's permitted tablet/screen time. Linking the mandatory wound care with a highly preferred activity ensures compliance, actively promoting natural drainage while eliminating the struggle of wound management.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None indicated.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Mandatory GP review in 48 hours to assess the wound for spreading cellulitis.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN
Call 000 or present to the nearest Emergency Department immediately if the child develops a high fever, uncontrollable shivering, or if angry red streaks begin spreading up the ankle and leg.

PATIENT INFORMATION
Disclaimer: Diagnosis is presumptive pending investigations; conditions are dynamic.
Clinical Course: The extreme sharp pain is now gone with the object removed. The foot will ache like a deep bruise for a few days, and the antibiotics will prevent the dirt from causing a deep infection.
SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE
Seek immediate medical review if the foot becomes significantly more swollen, hot to the touch, or begins draining yellow pus tomorrow.
PATIENT EDUCATION
Treatment Explanation: We removed the metal shard that was acting like a splinter, causing intense pain. The antibiotics are essential because the wire was dirty and puncture wounds seal over quickly, trapping bacteria inside.
Actionable Steps: Keep the child off their feet as much as possible tomorrow. Do the warm salt water soaks exactly as discussed. Ensure they take the full course of antibiotics even if the foot looks fully healed.

